NAME
       crontab - the format of cron's table of scheduled commands

SYNOPSIS
       crontab -e (each user's table)
       /etc/crontab, /etc/cron.d/* (system tables)

DESCRIPTION
       The cron service (crond) checks its tables every minute and runs each
       command whose time has come. Every user has a table, edited with
       crontab -e and listed with crontab -l; root can work on another
       user's with crontab -u USER. Those tables live in /var/spool/cron.

       Each line in a user's table is a schedule (five fields) and a command:

           MINUTE  HOUR  DAY-OF-MONTH  MONTH  DAY-OF-WEEK  COMMAND

       MINUTE         0-59
       HOUR           0-23
       DAY-OF-MONTH   1-31
       MONTH          1-12 (or jan-dec)
       DAY-OF-WEEK    0-7 (0 and 7 are Sunday; or sun-sat)

       In each field:
       *              every value
       5              exactly 5
       1-5            5 values, 1 to 5
       1,15,30        a list
       */10           every 10th value (0, 10, 20 ... for minutes)
       9-17/2         every 2nd value from 9 to 17

       When both DAY-OF-MONTH and DAY-OF-WEEK are restricted (not *), the
       command runs when EITHER matches.

       The command runs with /bin/sh in the user's home directory, with a
       small environment (PATH is only /usr/bin:/bin): write full paths
       for programs elsewhere. Lines like NAME=value set variables for the
       commands below them (SHELL, PATH, MAILTO). A % in the command means a
       newline; write \% for a real percent sign (date +\%F).

       Output a command prints is mailed to the user (if mail is set up);
       redirect it when you want to keep it.

       System tables (/etc/crontab and files in /etc/cron.d) have an extra
       sixth field before the command: the user to run it as.

   Shortcuts
       @reboot    once, when crond starts after boot
       @hourly    0 * * * *
       @daily     0 0 * * *
       @weekly    0 0 * * 0
       @monthly   0 0 1 * *
       @yearly    0 0 1 1 *

EXAMPLES
       Every day at 14:23:

           23 14 * * * /usr/local/bin/backup.sh

       Every 2 minutes, write a line to a log:

           */2 * * * * echo "cron is alive" >> /tmp/cron.log

       At 09:00 on weekdays (Monday to Friday):

           0 9 * * 1-5 /usr/bin/logger "Good morning"

       At 03:30 on the 1st of every month, in /etc/cron.d/cleanup (with a
       user field):

           30 3 1 * * root /usr/bin/find /tmp -mtime +30 -delete

       Give the user natasha a job, as root, without an editor:

           echo '30 12 * * * /bin/echo hello' | crontab -u natasha -
           crontab -l -u natasha

FILES
       /var/spool/cron/USER
              Each user's table (edit only with crontab -e).

       /etc/crontab, /etc/cron.d/
              System tables, with a user field.

       /etc/cron.hourly, cron.daily, cron.weekly, cron.monthly
              Scripts run at those intervals (by anacron for daily and up).

       /etc/cron.allow, /etc/cron.deny
              Who may use crontab.

SEE ALSO
       crontab(1), crond(8), at(1), systemd.timer(5)
